\C{joliputty} What's new in JoliPuTTY

JoliPuTTY is PuTTY with a new Windows front end: everything that
happens between you and the server (SSH, Telnet, Serial, the terminal
emulation, all the settings) is PuTTY's, and works as the rest of this
manual describes. What's different is the window around it, which is
made with WinUI 3, the user interface of Windows 11, and what that
window lets you do.

\H{jp-install} Installing and running JoliPuTTY

The JoliPuTTY program is called \cw{jputty.exe}. It needs Windows 11
(Windows 10 may work, but isn't the target), and the \i{Windows App
Runtime}, Microsoft's library for WinUI 3 applications:

\b If you installed JoliPuTTY as an MSIX package (from the Microsoft
Store, for instance), the Windows App Runtime comes with it, and
\cw{jputty} can be typed in a command prompt, with PuTTY's usual
command line (\k{using-cmdline}).

\b If you run \cw{jputty.exe} on its own, it needs the file
\cw{Microsoft.WindowsAppRuntime.Bootstrap.dll} beside it, and the
Windows App Runtime installed: if it isn't, JoliPuTTY offers to take
you to Microsoft's page to download it.

JoliPuTTY keeps its saved sessions where PuTTY does, in the Windows
registry, so PuTTY and JoliPuTTY see the same sessions, and you can go
back and forth between them.

PuTTYtel, pterm and the other tools (PSCP, PSFTP, Plink, Pageant and
PuTTYgen) are unchanged.

\H{jp-window} The main window

JoliPuTTY has a single window, with a \i{tab} for each session, in its
title bar, as in Windows Terminal or Notepad.

\S{jp-welcome} The Welcome tab

When JoliPuTTY starts without being told what to connect to, it opens
on a Welcome tab, which says how to start a session. It goes away by
itself when you start one. JoliPuTTY doesn't ask for confirmation when
you close it with only the Welcome tab open.

\S{jp-titlebar} The title bar

Beside the tabs are two buttons:

\dt The \q{+} button

\dd opens the configuration window on its Address page (\k{jp-address}),
with the cursor in the Host Name box: type where to connect to, and
press Enter. Hold Shift as you click it to open the new session in a
new window instead.

\dt The arrow button

\dd opens the pop-up of sessions (\k{jp-popup}). Its keyboard shortcut
is Ctrl+Shift+Space. Hold Shift as you choose a session in it to open
it in a new window.

Right-clicking the title bar, clicking the icon at its left, or
pressing Alt+Space, shows the window's menu, which has \q{Full Screen},
\q{Help} (this manual, on JoliPuTTY's web site, in the version for
the JoliPuTTY you're running) and \q{About JoliPuTTY}, along with the
usual commands to move, size and close the window.

\S{jp-popup} The pop-up of sessions

The pop-up that the arrow button opens lists your \i{favorite}
sessions, the ones you used most recently, and all your saved
sessions, in their folders (\k{jp-folders}). Click one to connect to
it in a new tab.

At its top is a box where you can type a host name to connect to
straight away, without going through the configuration window:
\cw{host}, \cw{user@host}, \cw{user@host:port}, or with the kind of
connection in front: \cw{ssh://host}, \cw{telnet://host}, or
\cw{serial://COM1:115200}, with the line's speed. Anything else is a
connection by SSH, with the default settings.

The pop-up can extend beyond the window if it needs the room.

\S{jp-tabs} Tabs

Each tab shows the title of its session. That's the title the server
has set, if it set one (most Unix shells set it to something like
\cw{user@host:~}); otherwise the window title from the session's
settings (\k{config-title}), if any; otherwise the name of the saved
session; and otherwise the host name. Hover over a tab to see its
session's full name, with its folder.

Double-clicking a tab opens its settings (\k{jp-midsession}).
Right-clicking it gives you its menu:

\dt \q{New Tab}

\dd opens the configuration window, to start a new session.

\dt \q{Rename Tab}

\dd lets you type a title of your own for the tab, in place of the
title of the session, for as long as the tab is open (it isn't saved
anywhere). Clear the title to go back to the session's own.

\dt \q{Move Left}, \q{Move Right}

\dd move the tab along the title bar. You can also drag tabs.

\dt \q{Duplicate Session}

\dd opens another tab, connected the same way.

\dt \q{Close Pane}

\dd (in a split tab: \k{jp-split}) closes the pane that has the focus.

\dt \q{Restart Session}

\dd connects again. If the session is still connected, JoliPuTTY asks
first.

\dt \q{Change Settings...}

\dd opens the session's settings (\k{jp-midsession}).

\dt \q{Event Log}

\dd shows the session's Event Log (\k{using-eventlog}). Its window can
be left open while you work, and you can copy lines from it.

\dt \q{Close Tab}

\dd closes the tab (Ctrl+F4). If the session is connected, JoliPuTTY
asks first, as PuTTY does when you close its window (see
\k{config-warnonclose}).

Ctrl+Tab and Ctrl+Shift+Tab go to the next and the previous tab.

\S{jp-split} Split panes

A tab can be split into several panes, side by side or one above the
other, each with a session of its own. \q{Split Right} and \q{Split
Down}, in the terminal's menu (\k{jp-termmenu}), split the pane you're
in, and open the configuration window for the new pane's session.
Drag the bar between two panes to share the room differently.

\q{Close Pane} closes the pane that has the focus; closing the last
one closes the tab.

\S{jp-termmenu} The terminal's menu

Right-clicking in the terminal (or Ctrl+right-clicking, depending on
the setting in \k{config-mouse}) gives you the terminal's menu:

\dt \q{Copy}, \q{Paste}

\dd copy the selected text, and paste what's in the clipboard.

\dt \q{Copy to Pool}, \q{Paste from Pool...}

\dd keep the selected text to paste again later, and paste it
(\k{jp-pools}).

\dt \q{Special Command}

\dd sends one of the special commands of the connection, such as
Telnet's Break or SSH's signals (\k{using-specials}).

\dt \q{Find...}

\dd finds text in the terminal and its scrollback: type it in the bar
that appears, and press Enter to go to the previous match (further up,
towards older output), Shift+Enter for the next one, and Escape to
close the bar. The bar can match case, and take a regular expression.

\dt \q{Send File...}, \q{Receive File...}

\dd copy a file to or from the server, by \i{SFTP}, over the SSH
connection that's already open (so there's nothing more to log in
to). They're available in SSH sessions whose server supports SFTP.

\dt \q{Split Right}, \q{Split Down}, \q{Close Pane}

\dd split the tab, or close a pane of it (\k{jp-split}).

\dt \q{Copy All to Clipboard}, \q{Clear Scrollback}, \q{Reset Terminal}

\dd are PuTTY's own (\k{using-copyall}, \k{reset-terminal}).

\H{jp-pools} Pools of copied text

JoliPuTTY keeps what you copy, to paste again later, in three
\i{pools}:

\b \e{The tab's history}: everything you copy in a tab goes there
automatically, and stays there as long as the tab is open.

\b \e{The session's pool}: text you keep for a saved session, with
\q{Copy to Pool} > \q{For this session}. It's there again whenever you
open that session.

\b \e{The common pool}: text you keep for all sessions, with \q{Copy
to Pool} > \q{For all sessions}.

\q{Paste from Pool...} (Ctrl+Alt+V) shows them all, the newest first,
with a box to find an entry by what's in it. Click an entry, or press
Enter, to paste it. Right-click an entry to paste it, keep it for this
session or for all of them, or remove it; and \q{Clear this tab's
history} empties the tab's history.

The session's pool and the common pool are saved in the registry,
encrypted for your Windows account only (with Windows' Data Protection
API). The tab's history is never saved.

The setting \q{Copies to keep} on the Terminal page says how many
entries the tab's history and the session's pool keep (20 by default;
0 to keep none, if what you copy in a session should never be kept).
Very long copies (over 16384 characters), and what \q{Copy All to
Clipboard} copies, aren't kept.

\H{jp-keys} Keyboard shortcuts

\dt Ctrl+Shift+Space

\dd opens the pop-up of sessions (\k{jp-popup}).

\dt Ctrl+comma

\dd opens the settings of the session you're in (\k{jp-midsession}).

\dt Ctrl+Tab, Ctrl+Shift+Tab

\dd go to the next or the previous tab.

\dt Ctrl+Alt+1 to Ctrl+Alt+8

\dd go to the first to the eighth tab; Ctrl+Alt+9 goes to the last one.

\dt Alt+arrow keys

\dd move to the pane to the left, the right, above or below, in a
split tab.

\dt Alt+Shift+plus, Alt+Shift+minus

\dd split the pane to the right, or down.

\dt Ctrl+Shift+W

\dd closes the pane that has the focus.

\dt Ctrl+Alt+V

\dd pastes from the pools (\k{jp-pools}).

\dt Ctrl+F4

\dd closes the tab.

These shortcuts use the left Alt key only, so that the right one (AltGr
on many keyboards) still types what it usually does.

If a program you run on the server needs some of these keys, tick
\q{Send them to the server instead}, under \q{JoliPuTTY's keyboard
shortcuts} on the Terminal page of the session's settings: JoliPuTTY
then leaves all of them to the server, except Ctrl+Tab and Ctrl+F4.

\H{jp-config} The configuration window

JoliPuTTY's configuration window has PuTTY's settings, and PuTTY's
pages, listed on its left, but works differently. The \q{?} button at
the end of its title bar opens this section of the manual.

\S{jp-sessions} The Sessions page

The configuration window opens on its Sessions page, which shows your
saved sessions in three sections: \e{Favorites}, \e{Recent}, the ones
you used last, and \e{All sessions}, in their folders. Each shows what
it connects to, under its name. Click a section's title to fold it
away.

At the top is a box where you can type a host name to connect to, as
in the pop-up of sessions (\k{jp-popup}).

Double-click a session to edit it (\k{jp-edit}). Its \q{...} button
has the rest of what you can do with it: connect to it (in this
window or in a new one), edit it, add it to your favorites or remove
it from them, rename it, duplicate it, export it, copy the command
line that would start it, or delete it. The \q{...} button of a folder
lets you start a new session or a new folder in it, rename it or
delete it.

The \q{...} button at the top of the page lets you start a new session
or a new folder, change the defaults for new sessions, and export all
your sessions to a \cw{.reg} file, or import sessions from one.

\S{jp-folders} Folders

Sessions can be put in \i{folders}, and folders in folders. JoliPuTTY
does it the way \i{PuTTY-ng} does, so the two see the same folders: a
session's folders are part of its name, separated by \q{#}. So the
session called \cw{Clients#ACME#web} is the session \cw{web}, in the
folder \cw{ACME}, in the folder \cw{Clients}. JoliPuTTY shows it with
\q{/} between the folders: \cw{Clients/ACME/web}. PuTTY itself shows
such a session under its full name.

That's why a session's or a folder's name can't contain \q{#}, \q{/}
or \q{\\}: JoliPuTTY doesn't let you type them.

A folder with nothing in it yet is remembered too, as it is by
PuTTY-ng. Renaming or deleting a folder renames or deletes everything
in it, after asking.

\S{jp-address} The Address page

The Address page is where you say where to connect to: the host name
and port, and the kind of connection, as on PuTTY's Session page
(\k{config-hostname}). With the rest of the settings at their
defaults, that's all a new session needs: press Connect.

\S{jp-edit} Editing a saved session

When you edit a saved session (or the defaults for new sessions), its
name is in the title bar of the window, with a dot and \q{Changes not
saved} as soon as you change anything. The pages you've changed
something on are marked, and so are the settings you've changed, each
with a button to put it back as it was.

\b \q{Save} saves the changes.

\b \q{Connect} connects to the session, with the changes, whether or
not you've saved them.

\b The \q{...} button can save it as a copy, rename it, delete it,
discard the changes, or start again from the defaults for new
sessions.

Saving a session under a new name (\q{Save as...}) asks for its name,
and for the folder to put it in, in a tree of your folders, where you
can also make a new one. Renaming or duplicating a session only
changes its name: it stays in its folder.

\S{jp-midsession} Changing the settings of a running session

Double-clicking a tab, \q{Change Settings...} in its menu, or
Ctrl+comma, opens the settings of its session. As in PuTTY (\k{using-changesettings}),
some settings can't be changed while the session's running: the
destination (host name, port, kind of connection) is then shown,
but greyed out. It can be changed once the session's over, to
restart it somewhere else.

\b \q{Apply} applies the changes to this session only.

\b \q{Apply and save} applies them, and saves them in the saved
session the tab was started from.

\b The \q{...} button has the same commands as when you edit a saved
session.

The Session page also lets you rename the saved session, and the
Connection page shows the SSH key and certificate the session logs in
with (\k{config-ssh-privkey}).

\S{jp-autologon} Automate logon

On the Connection > Data page, after the auto-login user name,
\q{Automate logon} lets you list steps to carry out when you connect,
by SSH or Telnet: wait for some text to arrive from the server (its
\q{Expected} text), then send a command, followed by Enter. With no
expected text, the command is sent as soon as the server has sent
anything. Each step can be switched off, and its command hidden, which
keeps it in the Windows Credential Manager rather than with the rest
of the session's settings.

\S{jp-config-moved} Where PuTTY's settings are

JoliPuTTY has all of PuTTY's settings, but some of them are somewhere
else than the rest of this manual says:

\b PuTTY's Session page is the Address page (\k{jp-address}); the
saved sessions are on the Sessions page (\k{jp-sessions}).

\b Logging (\k{config-logging}) is under Terminal > Logging.

\b \q{Close window on exit} (\k{config-closeonexit}) is on the Window
> Behaviour page, as \q{Close the tab when the session ends}.

\b The pages of SSH > Auth that matter most, Credentials and GSSAPI,
come first.

\b The theme (light, dark, or as Windows is), which is the same for
all sessions, is on the Window > Colours page. The \q{legacy} themes
don't colour the selected tab with the terminal's background colour.

\b The pools of copied text (\k{jp-pools}) and JoliPuTTY's keyboard
shortcuts (\k{jp-keys}) are on the Terminal page.

\b Automate logon (\k{jp-autologon}) is on the Connection > Data page.

The file, font and colour choosers, the Host CA dialog box, the Event
Log, and the messages and questions are JoliPuTTY's own, in the style
of Windows 11, and follow its light or dark theme:

\b The font chooser lists the fonts with a preview of each, and
shows the one you choose at the size you choose.

\b On the Window > Colours page, each colour in the list shows what
it is, and \q{Modify} opens a colour picker, where you can also type
its red, green and blue values.

\b The Event Log (\k{using-eventlog}) has its own window, which can
stay open while you work; select lines in it and press \q{Copy} to
copy them.

\H{jp-ssh} What servers see

JoliPuTTY identifies itself to SSH servers as \cw{JoliPuTTY_0.1}, so
that they can tell it from PuTTY (in their logs, for instance). It
behaves as the version of PuTTY it's based on, 0.85.
